{"type":"text","timestamp":"2026-10-17T17:38:59.290Z","data":{"text":"Both verifies are green:\n\n- Task verify `node --import tsx --test tests/lint-cargo-verify.test.ts` — 11/11 pass.\n- Proposal verify `pnpm verify` — exit 0 (typechecks, build, all tests, lint).\n\nSummary of what I did:\n\n**`src/core/spec/linter.ts`**\n- `analyzeVerifyCommand` gained an optional last parameter `runners`, defaulting to none; a command whose first word is a runner's binary and second word one of its subcommands now reports `unresolved: false`. `next-step.ts` is untouched.\n- `chainedVerifyError` now chooses between the package-script advice (root has `package.json`) and the `scripts/verify.sh` advice (it does not), per \"Chained verify refusal\".\n- `lintChangeFolder` reads `readLanguageMap` once, before the proposal verify check, and uses that single map for both `gatedTestPathFilter` and `verifyRunners`, plus a separate `pathExists` check for the root `package.json`.\n\n**`tests/lint-cargo-verify.test.ts`** (new) — one case per \"Cargo verify\" row, plus both chained-verify scenarios with exact spec strings; no git, no `cargo`.\n\n**`tests/lint-findings.test.ts`** — only the chained-verify expected string changed to the `scripts/verify.sh` advice.\n\nOne deviation worth the reviewer's attention (also recorded in the result file): the traceability `scenario` helper cannot be used for `spec-lint-and-approve`, because the living spec already contains a duplicate scenario name (\"Python-only repository\") that makes `lookupScenario` throw; I used plain `node:test`, as every other lint test does."}}
